 03_5	There is one analytic permission slip. Both series converge absolutely, so their product can be regrouped by diagonals without changing its sum. The diagonals are precisely the terms of E at the sum, x plus y. Therefore E of x times E of y equals E of the sum, x plus y. The multiplication law came from the factorials and convergence, not from naming the function exponential.
 03_6	Set y equal to minus x. The product is E of zero, which is one, so E never vanishes. For a real x, apply the same law to two copies of half x. E of x is the square of a nonzero real number, so it is positive. Since its derivative is itself, it is increasing. Those familiar properties now follow from what we built.
 03_7	Now we can name a number. Call E of one by the small letter e. The law says E of two is e times e, and E of three is e cubed. Roots give fractions, reciprocals give negatives, and continuity fills in the rest. That is when writing E of x as e to the x becomes a result, not a label.
+04_1	Return to the short straight steps. Each step took the current height and climbed by that height times the step's width. But climbing by a fraction of your own height is the same as multiplying your height. So a step one quarter wide multiplies by five quarters, whatever the height was. Four steps make five quarters to the fourth power, the endpoint we already drew.
+04_2	Give one unit of time this fixed width, and split it into n cells. Now let the slope be x times the current height. Over that one unit of time, the fractional rate x gives each of n steps the multiplier one plus x over n. After all the steps, we have that multiplier raised to the n-th power. For a rate of one, this is the familiar compound-growth construction of e. More cells keep raising its endpoint, by less and less.
+04_3	Now put those endpoints on the graph of our series. Change the rate, and the endpoint lands near the curve at that input, for rates above zero and below it. Near is still not a proof. Why should that limit be our series? Expand the finite product.
+04_4	Each cell offers two choices. Keep its one, or take its x over n. Multiplying out the product means making those choices in every possible way. With four cells, that is sixteen ways, and they sort themselves by how many cells took x. For x squared, two cells must take it, and six pairs of cells can do that. So the coefficient of x squared is six times a quarter times a quarter, six sixteenths.
+04_5	For any n and any k, count the same way. Pick cells one at a time. There are n options, then n minus one, then n minus two, until k cells are picked. Every set of k cells was counted k factorial times, so divide by k factorial. There is our factorial, earned a second way.
+04_6	Now each picked cell brings its one over n, and those share out across the picks. So the coefficient of x to the k is one over k factorial, multiplied by a short chain of correction factors. The chain runs one, one minus one over n, one minus two over n, and so on. For our six sixteenths, that is one half, times one, times three quarters. Ask for more cells than exist, and one factor is exactly zero.
+05_1	Hold k still, and let n grow. Here each bar shows how much of the series coefficient the product has reached. For any fixed k, every correction factor approaches one as n grows. The coefficient approaches exactly the coefficient in our series. The columns fill one at a time, but at every n, far columns are still nearly empty. If every column fills eventually, must the whole sum arrive?
+05_2	Matching each fixed coefficient is not enough by itself, because there are also more and more terms. Here is a sum where that reasoning fails. Take n terms, each equal to one over n. Every fixed term shrinks to zero, so column by column the limit looks like zero. Yet the total is one, for every n. The weight slips out to columns further and further away.
+05_3	Here is what makes our argument safe. Until k passes n, each correction factor lies between zero and one, and after that the chain is zero, so a whole chain never leaves that range. In absolute value, each finite-product term is no larger than the corresponding exponential-series term. These are the same ceilings we measured for the series, and n does not move them. Our warning sum has no such ceilings. Its column k can reach one over k, and those add up without limit.
+05_4	Now measure the distance to E of x. It is at most the total of the gaps between each bar and its ceiling. Say we want that distance below one hundredth. Choose a cut before choosing n. Beyond the cut, every gap fits under the series tail, for every n at once.
+05_5	We already know that series has a small tail, so push the cut out until the tail is small enough. Before the cut sit only finitely many columns, and each of their gaps closes. First make the shared tail small, then make the finitely many remaining coefficients close. That proves the whole expression converges.
+05_6	Nothing in that argument asked x to be positive. Slide to minus three halves. The odd terms turn negative, so the bars alternate up and down. In absolute value they are the same bars under the same ceilings. So the same cut and the same number of cells bound the distance here too.
+05_7	Push further, to minus three. One cell gives minus two. Two cells give one quarter, and three give exactly zero. Four cells give almost nothing, and then the values climb toward E of minus three. Decay needs a positive multiplier in every step, so the decay reading needs more than three cells. The algebra never asked for that. It never asked the values to climb steadily, and it never used a logarithm.
+05_8	So for every real x, that n-th power approaches E of x. For bigger inputs, this argument asks for a later cut and more cells, but every input arrives. A rate of one gives the number e, one special case of this limit. A rate of x for one unit of time and a rate of one for x units of time make the same product. So the stepped drawing out to any input reaches the curve at that input. That pays the second debt.
+06_1	We constructed a function whose derivative equals itself. Could a different function obey the same rule and start at the same height? What we just proved does not settle that. The steps reach E, but nothing yet forces every solution to follow our steps.
+06_2	So build a test. Take any function y that has a derivative, and multiply it by E of minus x. Differentiate that product. One contribution is y prime times E of minus x. The other comes from E of minus x itself, whose slope is minus E of minus x, by the chain rule. Together they make y prime minus y, times E of minus x.
+06_3	Try it on functions that break the rule. Our degree-two polynomial starts at height one, but its derivative loses its top term. Its product with E of minus x tilts. On these axes, degree four breaks the rule less, and tilts less. E of minus x is never zero, so the product can only be level where y prime equals y. Suppose a function obeys the rule everywhere. What must its product do?
+06_4	It stays level. One contribution is the proposed function times E of minus x, and the other is its negative. They cancel. A slope of zero across an interval means a constant value, and at zero that value is one. So y of x times E of minus x equals one, everywhere on that interval.
+06_5	Now multiply both sides by E of x. The product grid already gave us E of x times E of minus x equals one, so the left side collapses to y of x. The proposed solution must equal E of x everywhere on its interval. That is the rule in both directions. E obeys it, and whatever obeys it is E.
+06_6	Is this a circle? Follow the arrows. The series gave us E prime equals E, by differentiating term by term. Multiplying series in the grid gave us E of x times E of minus x equals one. Neither step used this result. All we added was the product rule, the chain rule, and the fact that zero slope on an interval means constant. No uniqueness theorem and no logarithm came in. Every arrow points one way.
+06_7	The word interval is doing work. Cut a gap in the domain, and the far piece can be three times E. Its slope equals its height, and the start at zero never hears about it. So the proposed function needs one unbroken interval containing the starting input, with a derivative at every point.
+06_8	The same test handles any constant rate k. If y prime equals k times y, multiply by E of minus k t. The contributions cancel again, so y of t equals y of zero times E of k t. A positive k grows, a negative k decays, a zero k stays put, and a zero start stays at zero. The rate k has units of one over time, so k t is a pure number, ready to be an input. Whether a real quantity obeys this law is a question for each application.
+06_9	The power series, the small-multiplier limit and the rate law are three constructions of the same object. Its powers, derivatives and growth rule fit together because they express the same structure from different sides. All three debts are paid.
